<p>
As for the second point, while working on hashing under various Common Lisp
predicates, I decided to bite the bullet and fix a few ugly parts of ECL. Most
notably consistent hashing of numbers under the predicate #'EQUALP. The topic is
interesting enough to deserve a separate note. In this post I'm assuming binary
floats (the float radix is two). This is the float representation used in ECL.
</p>

<div class="outline-2" id="outline-container-orgc9159c9">
<h2 id="orgc9159c9"><span class="section-number-2">1.</span> Hashing numbers - warmup</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-1">
</div>
<div class="outline-3" id="outline-container-org1e02788">
<h3 id="org1e02788"><span class="section-number-3">1.1.</span> Recap: hash table tests and hash functions</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-1-1">
<p>
A quick recap about Common Lisp and hash tables. The standard prescribes that
conforming implementations must allow specifying the hash table tests EQ, EQL,
EQUAL and EQUALP. The last test is interesting, because numbers with different
representations are considered to be EQUALP when they are =. For example:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-common-lisp"><code>(equalp 1 1.0)           <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">; -&gt; T
</span>(equalp 1/2 0.5)         <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">; -&gt; T
</span>(equalp 1/3 (float 1/3)) <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">; -&gt; NIL
</span>(equalp 42 #C(42.0 0.0)) <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">; -&gt; T</span>
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
It is worth noting, that hashing can be lossy, but it can't be inconsistent with
the test. In other words this is a conforming hash function under #'EQUALP:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-common-lisp"><code><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">(equalp 1 1.0)                        ; is true
</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">(= (hash-equalp 1) (hash-equalp 1.0)) ; is true
</span>
(<span style="color: #00bfff;">defun</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash-equalp</span> (x)
  (<span style="color: #00bfff;">typecase</span> x
    (real      42)
    (complex   (<span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (zerop (imagpart x))
                   (hash-equalp (realpart x))
                   13))
    (otherwise 15)))
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
While this function is not conforming, because there are objects that are the
same under the test and have different hashes:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-common-lisp"><code><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">(equalp 1 1.0)                        ; is true
</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">(= (hash-equalp 1) (hash-equalp 1.0)) ; is false
</span>
(<span style="color: #00bfff;">defun</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash-equalp</span> (x)
  (<span style="color: #00bfff;">typecase</span> x
    (rational  1337)
    (float     7117)
    (complex   (<span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (zerop (imagpart x))
                   (hash-equalp (realpart x))
                   13))
    (otherwise 15)))
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
In other words, when two objects are equal under the supplied test, they must
produce the same hash, but two objects having the same hash does not imply that
they are equal under the supplied test.
</p>

<p>
Naive hashes, while conforming, miss the point. A good hashing function tries to
produce different hashes for objects that are not equivalent under the predicate
with a good bit distribution to ensure good hash table performance.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="outline-3" id="outline-container-org6847aae">
<h3 id="org6847aae"><span class="section-number-3">1.2.</span> Recap: the numeric tower and mathematical equivalence</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-1-2">
<p>
Common Lisp has a full numeric tower:
</p>


<div class="figure" id="orgd45b374">
<p><img alt="numeric-tower.png" src="https://turtleware.eu/sxhash-equalp-files/numeric-tower.png" />
</p>
<p><span class="figure-number">Figure 5: </span>Common Lisp numeric tower.</p>
</div>

<p>
Note that when complex floats have specialized representation, then there may be
multiple subtypes specialized to single-float, double-float etc. Numbers are #'=
to each other when their value is mathematically the same, regardless of the
underlying representation. That's a wonderful quality when you do math :-).
</p>

<p>
This quality is not so wonderful, when you want to produce good hashes under the
predicate #'EQUALP (that boils down to #'= for numbers). We need to construct a
function that will produce the same hashes with good quality for equal objects.
</p>

<p>
The biggest hurdle comes from the gap between representations of RATIONAL and
FLOAT objects. Conventionally representations are
</p>

<dl class="org-dl">
<dt>fixnum</dt><dd>a single word with a few bits chopped to fit the tag</dd>
<dt>bignum</dt><dd>an array of "limbs", that is parts of the integer (plus sign)</dd>
<dt>ratio </dt><dd>a pair of two integers</dd>
<dt>float </dt><dd>a pair of N-bit mantissa and K-bit exponent (plus sign)</dd>
<dt>(complex rational)</dt><dd>a pair of two rationals</dd>
<dt>(complex float)   </dt><dd>a pair of two floats of the same type</dd>
</dl>

<p>
Fixnums, bignums and ratios are disjoint under #'=. That leaves us with
questions:
</p>

<ol class="org-ol">
<li><p>
When are two complex numbers mathematically equal?
</p>

<p>
This one is easy. Two complex numbers are equal when their real parts are
equal and their imaginary parts are equal.
</p></li>

<li><p>
When are two floats mathematically equal?
</p>

<p>
To resolve this question we can cast the float with smaller precision to the
float with bigger one and compare bits. There is a gotcha in that -0.0 is not
EQL to +0.0, but it is always = to it, so we should canonicalize zeros first.
</p></li>

<li><p>
When is an integer mathematically equal to a float?
</p>

<p>
When the float has no fractional part and integers are equal.
</p></li>

<li><p>
When is a ratio equal by #'= to a float?
</p>

<p>
This is the most interesting question. To answer it, we need to dive a bit
deeper into float representation.
</p>

<p>
Finite binary float values conceptually are represented as:
</p>

<p>
<code>(* sign mantissa (expt 2 exponent))</code>
</p>

<p>
This corresponds to <i>dyadic rationals</i>, that is rational numbers whose
denominator is a power of two:
</p>

<p>
<code>(/ n (expt 2 k))</code>
</p>

<p>
In other words a float and a ratio may be equal only when the ratio is
dyadic, otherwise the ratio value can't be represented as a binary float
(this post assumes that we operate on binary floats - with radix=2).
</p></li>
</ol>

<p>
That sums up the recap about mathematical equivalence and numeric tower in
Common Lisp.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="outline-3" id="outline-container-orgcc320c4">
<h3 id="orgcc320c4"><span class="section-number-3">1.3.</span> Establishing the baseline</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-1-3">
<p>
The easiest, yet useless, approach is to always return a hash <code>42</code>. It is
adequate because that is the canonical answer to the most important question.
</p>

<p>
The easy and useful approach relies on the observation that hashing is lossy.
All real numbers are coerced to a float precision and hashed. When the rational
number does not fit in the float precision, then we hash its approximation. We
need to remember to canonicalize -0.0 to +0.0 and to disable floating point
exceptions for cases like overflows and underflows.
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-c"><code><span style="color: #ffd700;">#define</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash_single_float</span>(<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>,<span style="color: #4eee94;">v</span>) hash_float((h), (<span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span>)(v))
<span style="color: #ffd700;">#define</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash_double_float</span>(<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>,<span style="color: #4eee94;">v</span>) hash_float((h), (<span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span>)(v))

<span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #00bfff;">inline</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_float</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">v</span>)
{
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (v==-0.0) v=0.0;
  hash_data(h, &amp;v, <span style="color: #00bfff;">sizeof</span>(v));
}

<span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #00bfff;">inline</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_long_float</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">long</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">v</span>)
{
  ECL_WITHOUT_FPE_BEGIN {
    hash_float(h, (<span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span>)(v));
  } ECL_WITHOUT_FPE_END;
}

<span style="color: #ffd700;">#define</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash_fixnum</span>(<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>,<span style="color: #4eee94;">x</span>) hash_float((h),(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span>)ecl_fixnum(x))
<span style="color: #ffd700;">#define</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash_bignum</span>(<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>,<span style="color: #4eee94;">x</span>) hash_rational((h),(x))
<span style="color: #ffd700;">#define</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash_ratio</span>(<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>,<span style="color: #4eee94;">x</span>)  hash_rational((h),(x))

<span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #00bfff;">inline</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_rational</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">x</span>)
{
  ECL_WITHOUT_FPE_BEGIN {
    <span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">v</span> = ecl_to_double(x);
    hash_float(h, v);
  } ECL_WITHOUT_FPE_END;
}
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
This model is conceptually obvious, very maintainable, reasonably fast, and
there are relatively few collisions under the usual workload. We pay for it in a
few ways:
</p>

<ol class="org-ol">
<li>Aesthetically it is not pleasing - crude even.</li>

<li>"Relatively few collisions" applies only to toy examples, once we start using
bigger integers and more precise ratios, the problem starts to show. For
example 2<sup>60</sup> and 2<sup>60</sup>+1 hash identically and that matters for 64bit IDs and
timestamps.</li>

<li>Performance-wise we drop the ball for integers and rationals &ndash; we convert
numbers between formats even when we could hash only a chunk of memory.</li>
</ol>

<p>
This list is ranked by importance as I see it. The main reason I've kept digging
deeper is that it was not to my taste. Then we had impracticality for serious
workloads, finally microoptimizations. I believe that the general audience has
these priorities in a reversed order.
</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-2" id="outline-container-orgf18e8f8">
<h2 id="orgf18e8f8"><span class="section-number-2">2.</span> Hashing numbers - round one</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-2">
<p>
A harder but more precise way to hash numbers under the predicate #'EQUALP is to
introduce canonicalization rules:
</p>

<ol class="org-ol">
<li><p>
Integers are hashed as integers
</p>

<p>
In the case of FIXNUM we hash its value (a word without the tag) and in the
case of BIGNUM we hash the sign and its limbs.
</p></li>

<li><p>
Finite floats without the fractional part are hashed as integers
</p>

<p>
Ensures INTEGER &lt;-&gt; FLOAT correspondence. We can't cast LONG-FLOAT to
DOUBLE-FLOAT and then cast to integer, because we'd miss many BIGNUM values.
Caveat: with this approach big floats may cons full bignums.
</p></li>

<li><p>
Floats that are not integers are cast to DOUBLE-FLOAT and hashed
</p>

<p>
Ensures X-FLOAT &lt;-&gt; Y-FLOAT correspondence. This approach is lossy for long
floats if they have more bits than double floats, but simplifies the
implementations because long double has a few "loose" bits that may differ
between numbers with the same value.
</p>

<p>
NaNs and infinities are hashed like other doubles because infinities are
exact and NaN is never #'= to anything.
</p></li>

<li><p>
Dyadic ratios are hashed as DOUBLE-FLOAT
</p>

<p>
Ensures FLOAT &lt;-&gt; RATIO correspondence. For simplicity we don't try to
determine whether RATIO is representable as DOUBLE-FLOAT &ndash; if it is too big
or too small, it will be represented either as a 0 or as an infinity.
</p></li>

<li><p>
Other ratios are hashed as pairs
</p>

<p>
We hash the numerator and denominator separately.
</p></li>

<li><p>
Complex floats with <code>(ZEROP (IMAGPART X))</code> are hashed as <code>(HASH (REALPART X))</code>
</p>

<p>
Because <code>(= 3 #c(3.0 0.0))</code> we need to canonicalize <code>(complex float)</code> while
rational complex numbers are canonicalized implicitly. Other complexes are
hashed as pairs, that is we hash the real part and imaginary part separately.
</p></li>
</ol>

<p>
This approach results in fewer collisions and is fairly straightforward. Here is
the pseudocode:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-common-lisp"><code>(<span style="color: #00bfff;">defun</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash-equalp-number</span> (state number)
  (<span style="color: #00bfff;">typecase</span> number
    (fixnum  (hash-word state number))
    (bignum  (hash-data state (signum number) (limbs number)))
    (float   (<span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (and (finitep number) (is-integer-p number))
                 (hash-equalp-number state (rational number))
                 <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">INV -0.0 and +0.0 are hashed as integers.
</span>                 (hash-data state (coerce number 'double-float))))
    (ratio   (<span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (dyadic-ratio-p number)
                 <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">INV may produce infinity or zero (that's fine)
</span>                 <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Disable float overflow and underflow traps.
</span>                 (hash-data state (coerce number 'double-float))
                 (<span style="color: #00bfff;">progn</span>
                   (hash-equalp-number state (numerator number))
                   (hash-equalp-number state (denominator number)))))
    (complex (<span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (zerop (imagpart number))
                 (hash-equalp-number state (realpart number))
                 (<span style="color: #00bfff;">progn</span>
                   (hash-equalp-number state (realpart number))
                   (hash-equalp-number state (imagpart number)))))))
</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="outline-3" id="outline-container-org74c831a">
<h3 id="org74c831a"><span class="section-number-3">2.1.</span> Implementation bites</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-2-1">
<p>
My initial implementation cast all floats to integers. When I was comparing ECL
to other implementations I was wondering why we are so slow compared to others.
The key insight came after the realization that randomized keys in the form
(random 20.0) were collapsed to only 20 distinct hashes.
</p>

<p>
Fixing that and giving non-integer floats distinct hashes gave ECL the most
noticeable boost on the HTABLE-MIXBAG benchmark (included at the beginning) -
the new version is 100x faster than the old one.
</p>

<p>
While implementing a test suite that ensured that hashes for bignums, dyadic
ratios, and corresponding floats matched, I've discovered a non-obvious bug
pertaining to casting double floats to integers. The code looked like this:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-c"><code><span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (d&lt;=MOST_POSITIVE_FIXNUM &amp;&amp; d&gt;=MOST_NEGATIVE_FIXNUM) <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span> to_fixnum(d);
<span style="color: #00bfff;">else</span>                                                    <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span> to_bignum(d);
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
But the <code>MOST_POSITIVE_FIXNUM</code> value on x86-64 is 2<sup>61</sup>-1. In other words, to
represent this number exactly as a double, we need 61 significant bits. That's a
shame because double has 53 significant bits, so <code>MOST_POSITIVE_FIXNUM</code>, when
converted to double, rounds to 2<sup>61</sup> and that can't fit in a fixnum. After
promotion the incorrect code is equivalent to:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-c"><code><span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (d&lt;=(1LL&lt;&lt;61) &amp;&amp; d&gt;=MOST_NEGATIVE_FIXNUM) <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span> to_fixnum(d);
<span style="color: #00bfff;">else</span>                                         <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span> to_bignum(d);
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
Oops, double is accidentally cast to fixnum when it is 2<sup>61</sup>. That's clearly a
problem! After finding the issue, the solution is easy. We need to find an exact
double that is the boundary. Note that integers are implemented as two's
complement, so the most negative fixnum was alright in our case:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-c"><code><span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">limit</span> = ldexp(1.0, ECL_FIXNUM_BITS-ECL_TAG_BITS-1);
<span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (d&lt;limit &amp;&amp; d&gt;=-limit) <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span> to_fixnum(d);
<span style="color: #00bfff;">else</span>                      <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span> to_bignum(d);
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
This fix is correct, yet it revealed another bug. But that's a different story
to tell.
</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-2" id="outline-container-org1e8be0b">
<h2 id="org1e8be0b"><span class="section-number-2">3.</span> Hashing numbers - round two</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-3">
<p>
The approach above is correct and nice, but has a few important flaws:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>we cons a lot for big integer floats (2KB/float in the worst case)</li>
<li>there is a whole class of dyadic ratios that don't fit in long-float</li>
<li>long floats lose precision after cast to double, notably very small and very
big values collapse to 0 and infinity</li>
</ul>

<p>
After the initial implementation it bugged me enough that I've decided to come
up with a new hashing scheme for numbers under the predicate EQUALP. The rules
are also simpler (at least conceptually):
</p>

<ol class="org-ol">
<li>Dyadic rationals are hashed as tuples (sign, mantissa, exponent)</li>
<li>Other numbers are hashed depending on their type (they are disjoint)</li>
</ol>

<p>
The representation <code>[SIGN MANTISSA EXPONENT]</code> does not require us to convert
between types and it is very compact. Moreover it is very precise and doesn't
produce unnecessary collisions. On the other hand it requires extra work to
normalize tuples. Here is the pseudocode:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-common-lisp"><code>(<span style="color: #00bfff;">defun</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash-equalp-number</span> (state number)
  (<span style="color: #00bfff;">typecase</span> number
    (integer
     (<span style="color: #00bfff;">multiple-value-bind</span> (s m e) (compute-integer-dyad number)
       (hash-dyad state s m e)))
    (float
     (<span style="color: #00bfff;">multiple-value-bind</span> (s m e) (compute-float-dyad number)
       (hash-dyad state s m e)))
    (ratio
     (<span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (dyadic-ratio-p number)
         (<span style="color: #00bfff;">multiple-value-bind</span> (s m e) (compute-ratio-dyad number)
           (hash-dyad state s m e))
         (<span style="color: #00bfff;">progn</span>
           (hash-datatype-tag state <span style="color: #f08080;">:ratio</span>)
           (hash-equalp-number state (numerator number))
           (hash-equalp-number state (denominator number)))))
    (complex
     (<span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (zerop (imagpart number))
         (hash-equalp-number state (realpart number))
         (<span style="color: #00bfff;">progn</span>
           (hash-datatype-tag state <span style="color: #f08080;">:complex</span>)
           (hash-equalp-number state (realpart number))
           (hash-equalp-number state (imagpart number)))))))
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
The implementation is a bit less straightforward because we now need to be able
to compute mantissa from numbers. A few words about normalization:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>sign is -1, 0 or +1 for finite numbers - that solves normalization of -0.0/+0.0</li>
<li>mantissa must be a positive odd integer or zero - that unifies all mantissas</li>
<li>exponent is also an integer, so the number is (* sign mantissa (expt 2 exp))</li>
<li>to hash unique values (like infinities) we will abuse sign to fit extra values</li>
</ul>

<p>
Every integer is a dyadic rational, so our task is to normalize it so that the
mantissa is an odd number and adjust the exponent accordingly.
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-common-lisp"><code>(<span style="color: #00bfff;">defun</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">compute-integer-dyad</span> (number <span style="color: #98f5ff;">&amp;optional</span> (exp 0))
  (<span style="color: #00bfff;">when</span> (zerop number)
    (<span style="color: #00bfff;">return-from</span> compute-integer-dyad
      (values 0 0 0)))
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">In the real code use COUNT-TRAILING-ZEROS + ASH.
</span>  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Loop division is to make the mechanism clear.
</span>  (<span style="color: #00bfff;">do</span> ((sign (signum number))
       (mantissa (abs number) (ash mantissa -1))
       (exponent exp (1+ exponent)))
      ((oddp mantissa)
       (values sign mantissa exponent))))
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
The implementation for float is also straightforward thanks to the function
INTEGER-DECODE-FLOAT. We also need to normalize the signed zero:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-common-lisp"><code>(<span style="color: #00bfff;">defun</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">compute-float-dyad</span> (number)
  (<span style="color: #00bfff;">cond</span> ((nan-p number)
         (<span style="color: #00bfff;">return-from</span> compute-float-dyad
           (values -2 0 0)))
        ((negative-infinity-p number)
         (<span style="color: #00bfff;">return-from</span> compute-float-dyad
           (values -3 0 0)))
        ((positive-infinity-p number)
         (<span style="color: #00bfff;">return-from</span> compute-float-dyad
           (values +3 0 0))))
  (<span style="color: #00bfff;">multiple-value-bind</span> (mantissa exponent sign)
      (integer-decode-float number)
    (compute-integer-dyad (* sign mantissa) exponent)))
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
For ratios we will reuse the function that we have <span class="underline">not</span> defined in the last
section to decide whether the ratio is dyadic and if it is - convert it to the
canonical form:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-common-lisp"><code>(<span style="color: #00bfff;">defun</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">dyadic-ratio-p</span> (number)
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">A positive integer is a power of 2 only when exactly one bit is set.
</span>  (= 1 (logcount (denominator number))))

(<span style="color: #00bfff;">defun</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">compute-ratio-dyad</span> (number)
  (values (signum number)
          <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Ratios are already normalized(!)
</span>          (abs (numerator number))
          <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">;; </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">den = 2^k, shift = k+1 -&gt; exponent = -k
</span>          (- (1- (integer-length (denominator number))))))
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
Mind that the functions above are deliberately simple to illustrate how they
should work. We have also used non-standard functions NAN-P and INFINITY-P that
depend on the implementations extensions (or internals).
</p>

<p>
This is the most satisfying moment in the post - we have found an elegant and
universal (in this domain) abstraction that avoids float-to-bignum conversion,
hashes unequal numbers differently, and is easy to understand. That's the high
point of this post and the reason I've dubbed it "heavenly delights". Alas, it
is time to come back to earth and improve its performance. That's where the
engineering lives.
</p>
</div>
<div class="outline-3" id="outline-container-org4b7d2a6">
<h3 id="org4b7d2a6"><span class="section-number-3">3.1.</span> Back to earth</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-3-1">
<p>
We have an elegant conceptual model. The thing with elegant conceptual models is
that they need to be dirtied to make them fast. And that's what we are going to
do now: we will bastardize the clean abstraction and make it performant under
usual scenarios without sacrificing precision. We are now optimizing.
</p>

<p>
Simplifications that we can climb down to from our normalized representation
[sign, mantissa, exponent] model:
</p>

<ol class="org-ol">
<li><p>
When mantissa or exponent exceeds LONG-FLOAT precision, don't normalize the
number
</p>

<p>
As noted earlier, aside from floats, numbers in Common Lisp are disjoint, for
example, there are no integers and ratios that are EQUALP to each other. In
that case we use the most convenient hashing scheme for the specified type.
</p></li>

<li><p>
Dyads representable as fixnums are hashed as a fixnum (a signed integer)
</p>

<p>
This is an opportunistic optimization tailored for quick fixnum hashing. We
already know how to correctly determine when a float can be reliably cast to
fixnum and other numbers don't intersect with fixnums.
</p></li>

<li><p>
Dyadic ratios in range are cast to <code>long double</code> and hashed as LONG-FLOAT
</p>

<p>
This way we ensure that ratios and floats that are #'= are hashed the same.
This is similar to "Round one" approach, but we retain the maximal precision.
</p></li>

<li><p>
Integer floats and bignums in range are hashed in a dyadic form
</p>

<p>
This step allows us to avoid coercing floats to bignums and is a big memory
allocation save. A small cost for bignums is that we need to decide before
hashing whether it is in the LONG-FLOAT precision range.
</p></li>

<li><p>
Long floats exceeding double float precision are hashed in a dyadic form
</p>

<p>
By avoiding lossy casts to double, we reduce number of collisions. Most
notably, very small and very big values are not collapsed to zero and
infinity.  Such floats are hashed in a dyadic form because x87 floats have
additional padding bits that may differ between equal values, so we can't
hash memory.
</p></li>
</ol>

<p>
Another minor optimization: we fold the sign and the exponent into a single word
to have fewer hash state updates as <code>8*exponent+sign</code> (sign has maximum 2 bits).
</p>

<p>
Notice that this scheme is very similar to the "Round one" approach. In practice
it only differs in how we treat floats, bignums and ratios:
