<p>
In two places we will hash integers that may be either fixnums or bignums:
</p>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>the mantissa in a dyadic form (long double mantissa is 64bit on x86-64)</li>
<li>a ratio's numerator and denominator when the ratio is not a dyadic number</li>
</ul>

<p>
Hashing them is straightforward. Note that hashing the fixnum is always cheap -
we hash a single word.
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-c"><code><span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #00bfff;">inline</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_fixnum_data</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">x</span>) {
  hash_word(h, ecl_fixnum(x));
}

<span style="color: #ffd700;">#define</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash_fixnum</span>(<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>,<span style="color: #4eee94;">x</span>) hash_fixnum_data(h,x)

<span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #00bfff;">inline</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_bignum_data</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">x</span>) {
  hash_word(h, ecl_bigsgn(x));
  hash_data(h,
            ECL_BIGNUM_LIMBS(x),
            ECL_BIGNUM_USIZE(x) * <span style="color: #00bfff;">sizeof</span>(ecl_limb_t));
}

<span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_integer</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">x</span>)
{
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (ECL_FIXNUMP(x)) {
    hash_fixnum_data(h, x);
  } <span style="color: #00bfff;">else</span> {
    hash_bignum_data(h, x);
  }
}
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
Previously we were concerned with consing. This is why we go an extra mile to
not allocate bignums unnecessarily when we hash a bignum in a dyadic form. We
operate on a scratch register that is a thread-local variable in ECL when we
extract a mantissa:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-c"><code><span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #00bfff;">inline</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_and_free_bignum_register</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">mantissa</span>) {
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">INV this function assumes that mantissa is already positive. The bignum may
     not be normalized to fixnum. We free the register after hashing.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (ECL_BIGNUM_SIZE(mantissa) == 1) {
    <span style="color: #98f5ff;">ecl_limb_t</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">y</span> = ECL_BIGNUM_LIMBS(mantissa)[0];
    <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (y &lt;= MOST_POSITIVE_FIXNUM) {
      hash_word(h, y);
      <span style="color: #00bfff;">goto</span> <span style="color: #a2cd5a;">DONE</span>;
    }
  }
  hash_bignum_data(h, mantissa);
 <span style="color: #a2cd5a;">DONE</span>:
  _ecl_big_register_free(mantissa);
}

<span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">When hashing bignums we see whether they fit in LONG-FLOAT and only then we
   cast it to a dyadic form. Otherwise we directly hash limbs.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
<span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_bignum</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">x</span>) {
  <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_fixnum</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">length</span> = _ecl_big_integer_length(x);
  <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_fixnum</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">exponent</span> = _ecl_big_ctz(x);
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (length-exponent&lt;=LDBL_MANT_DIG &amp;&amp; length&lt;=LDBL_MAX_EXP) {
    <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">To avoid allocating fresh bignums by ABS and ASH we opencode this:
       cl_object mantissa = ecl_ash(ecl_abs(x), -exponent);</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
    <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">reg</span> = _ecl_big_register2();
    <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_fixnum</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">signum</span> = ecl_bigsgn(x);
    <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (signum&lt;0) {
      _ecl_big_neg(reg, x);
      _ecl_big_div_2exp(reg, reg, exponent);
    }
    <span style="color: #00bfff;">else</span> {
      _ecl_big_div_2exp(reg, x, exponent);
    }
    hash_word(h, 8*exponent+signum);
    hash_and_free_bignum_register(h, reg);
  } <span style="color: #00bfff;">else</span> {
    hash_bignum_data(h, x);
  }
}
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
Hashing a ratio is also plain. When the ratio is dyadic and its precision does
not exceed <code>long double</code> then we coerce it to that type and hash it. Otherwise
we add the tag and hash both integers. We are careful to not allocate memory.
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-c"><code><span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_ratio</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">x</span>) {
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">When the ratio is dyadic and within long-float precision, then we cast it
     to long double for consistent hashes. Otherwise we hash ratio as is.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">num</span> = ecl_ratio_num(x);
  <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">den</span> = ecl_ratio_den(x);
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Ratio is dyadic when denominator is a power of two. To avoid bignum
     arithmetic we provide an optimized path for each subtype of integer.
     ecl_zerop(ecl_boole(ECL_BOOLAND, den, ecl_one_minus(den)));</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  <span style="color: #98f5ff;">bool</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">dyadic_p</span>;
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (ECL_FIXNUMP(den)) {
    <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Denominator is always positive, so fixnump(x) -&gt; fixnump(x-1)</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
    <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_fixnum</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">dfix</span> = ecl_fixnum(den);
    dyadic_p = !(dfix &amp; (dfix-1));
  } <span style="color: #00bfff;">else</span> {
    <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">When bignum is a pow2, then integer_length == ctz+1 or (popcount==1)</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
    dyadic_p = ((_ecl_big_ctz(den)+1) == _ecl_big_integer_length(den));
  }
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (dyadic_p) {
    <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Normally we would call here
       cl_fixnum length = ecl_integer_length(ecl_abs(num));

       But! Numerator is normalized to an odd integer because denominator is a
       power of two. INTEGER-LENGTH returns different values for X and -X only
       when X is a power of two, and the only odd power of two is X=1.

       (INTEGER-LENGTH -1) == 0 and 0 &lt;= LDBL_MANT_DIG
       (INTEGER-LENGTH +1) == 1 and 1 &lt;= LDBL_MANT_DIG

       This is why we don't need to call ABS on the numerator. QED.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
    <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_fixnum</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">length</span> = (ECL_FIXNUMP(num)
                        ? ecl_integer_length(num)
                        : _ecl_big_integer_length(num));
    <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_fixnum</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">exponent</span> = -(ecl_integer_length(den)-1);
    <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (length &lt;= LDBL_MANT_DIG &amp;&amp; exponent &gt;= LDBL_MIN_EXP-LDBL_MANT_DIG) {
      <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Generic way to do this is: hash_long_float(h, ecl_to_long_double(x));
         that path recomputes mantissa and exponent from ratio using a generic
         arithmetic. We already have everything ready.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
      <span style="color: #98f5ff;">long</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">value</span> = (ECL_FIXNUMP(num)
                           ? ecl_fixnum(num)
                           : _ecl_big_get_lf(num));
      value = ldexpl(value, exponent);
      hash_long_float(h, value);
      <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span>;
    }
  }
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Fall-through.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  hash_word(h, t_ratio);
  hash_integer(h, num);
  hash_integer(h, den);
}
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
Hashing floats is the most demanding part in this program:
</p>

<ol class="org-ol">
<li>DOUBLE-FLOAT: the first branch ensures the same hashes for fixnums, bignums,
and integer float types, while the second branch ensures the same hashes for
ratios and fractional float types.</li>

<li>LONG-FLOAT: the first branch checks whether we can cast to double without
losing precision. The second branch ensures same hashes for fixnums. The
third branch unconditionally hashes the value in a dyadic form.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Initially I've introduced functions <code>decode_lf</code> and <code>decode_df</code> that extracted
mantissa and exponent from the number, but benchmarks showed that these small
allocations dominate both time and consing for relevant profiles. This is why
I've fused decoding and hashing and operate on a preallocated register. That's
the difference between coming up first and coming up third on the benchmark.
</p>

<p>
Decoding and hashing <code>double</code> and <code>long double</code> in a dyadic form are practically
the same, so I'm going to show only <code>hash_lfloat</code> version. Both functions have a
fast path when the mantissa fits in <code>cl_index</code>, and a portable slow path when it
doesn't. Initially I've picked <code>unsigned long long</code>, but this type can't be
directly assigned to a bignum in GMP, so I've decided on a more robust type.
</p>

<p>
Functions <code>hash_dfloat</code> and <code>hash_lfloat</code> are identical except for
<code>DBL_MANT_DIG</code> -&gt; <code>LDBL_MANT_DIG</code>, <code>ldexp</code> -&gt; <code>ldexpl</code> and <code>frexp</code> -&gt;
<code>frexpl</code>. Common Lisp, with its organic macros, could save us repetition and
expand a single macro twice, but C preprocessor macros get hairy fast, so it is
better to copy-paste the code:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-c"><code><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">INV value is guaranteed to be finite and != 0.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>

<span style="color: #ffd700;">#define</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">DECODE_SIGNUM</span>(<span style="color: #4eee94;">value</span>,<span style="color: #4eee94;">signum</span>) \
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">do</span> { <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (signbit(value)) { signum=-1; value=-value; } } <span style="color: #00bfff;">while</span>(0)

<span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">... hash_dfloat(cl_hashstate *h, double value) ...</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>

<span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #00bfff;">inline</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_lfloat</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">long</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">value</span>)
{
<span style="color: #ffd700;">#if</span> LDBL_MANT_DIG &lt;= ECL_FIXNUM_BITS
  <span style="color: #98f5ff;">int</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">ctz</span>, <span style="color: #4eee94;">exponent</span>, <span style="color: #4eee94;">signum</span>=1;
  <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_index</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">mantissa</span>;
  DECODE_SIGNUM(value, signum);
  value = ldexpl(frexpl(value, &amp;exponent), LDBL_MANT_DIG);
  exponent -= LDBL_MANT_DIG;
  mantissa = (<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_index</span>)value;
  ctz = ecl_ctz(mantissa);
  exponent += ctz;
  mantissa &gt;&gt;= ctz;
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">binary80 mantissa is 64 bits so it can falsify &lt;= MOST_POSITIVE_FIXNUM.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (mantissa &lt;= MOST_POSITIVE_FIXNUM) {
    hash_word(h, 8*exponent+signum);
    hash_word(h, mantissa);
  } <span style="color: #00bfff;">else</span> {
    <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">reg</span> = _ecl_big_register2();
    _ecl_big_set_idx(reg, mantissa);
    hash_word(h, 8*exponent+signum);
    hash_bignum_data(h, reg);
    _ecl_big_register_free(reg);
  }
<span style="color: #ffd700;">#else</span> <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Happens for 128bit floats (i.e aarch64).</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">const</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">int</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">fixnum_bits</span> = ECL_FIXNUM_BITS-ECL_TAG_BITS-1;
  <span style="color: #98f5ff;">int</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">ctz</span>=0, <span style="color: #4eee94;">exponent</span>, <span style="color: #4eee94;">signum</span>=1;
  <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_object</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">mantissa</span> = _ecl_big_register2();
  <span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_fixnum</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">helper</span>, <span style="color: #4eee94;">iterator</span>;
  DECODE_SIGNUM(value, signum);
  value = frexpl(value, &amp;exponent);
  _ecl_big_set_ui(mantissa, 0);
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">for</span>(iterator=0; iterator&lt;LDBL_MANT_DIG &amp;&amp; value; iterator+=fixnum_bits) {
    value = ldexpl(value, fixnum_bits);
    helper = (<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_fixnum</span>)value;
    value -= helper;
    _ecl_big_mul_2exp(mantissa, mantissa, fixnum_bits);
    _ecl_big_add_ui(mantissa, mantissa, helper);
    <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">When this chunk contains only 0s, then we extend trailing zeros by
       fixnum_bits (chunk's size). Otherwise we start counting again.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
    ctz = !helper ? ctz+fixnum_bits : ecl_ctz(helper);
  }
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">ctz is a trailing 0 count for the whole mantissa. iterator denotes how many
     bits we have shifted the mantissa.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  exponent += ctz - iterator;
  _ecl_big_div_2exp(mantissa, mantissa, ctz);
  hash_word(h, 8*exponent+signum);
  hash_and_free_bignum_register(h, mantissa);
<span style="color: #ffd700;">#endif</span>
}
<span style="color: #ffd700;">#undef</span> DECODE_SIGNUM
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
At last we can hash some floats. Hashing single floats is trivial (we cast them
to double float) while hashing a long float requires more work:
</p>

<dl class="org-dl">
<dt>integer path</dt><dd>we need to extract the mantissa from it separately because it
can store bignums outside of a double float range.</dd>

<dt>fraction path</dt><dd>DOUBLE-FLOAT is hashed as is, LONG-FLOAT are hashed as a dyad
unless it can be represented as DOUBLE-FLOAT, in that case it is cast to it</dd>
</dl>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-c"><code><span style="color: #ffd700;">#define</span> <span style="color: #daa520;">hash_single_float</span>(<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>,<span style="color: #4eee94;">v</span>) hash_double_float((h),(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span>)(v))

<span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_double_float</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">value</span>) {
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (isfinite(value) &amp;&amp; (value==trunc(value))) {
    <span style="color: #00bfff;">const</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">limit</span> = ldexp(1.0, ECL_FIXNUM_BITS-ECL_TAG_BITS-1);
    <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (value &lt; limit &amp;&amp; value &gt;= -limit) {
      hash_word(h, (<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_fixnum</span>)value);
      <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span>;
    }
    hash_dfloat(h, value);
    <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span>;
  }
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Fall-through.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  hash_data(h, &amp;value, <span style="color: #00bfff;">sizeof</span>(value));
}

<span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">We need to ensure, that when long double can be represented as double without
   loss of precision, that is value == (double)value, to hash it as double.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
<span style="color: #00bfff;">static</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">void</span>
<span style="color: #daa520;">hash_long_float</span>(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_hashstate</span> *<span style="color: #4eee94;">h</span>, <span style="color: #98f5ff;">long</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">value</span>) {
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Hash as double.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> (!isfinite(value)
      || (fabsl(value) &lt;= DBL_MAX &amp;&amp; value==(<span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span>)value) ) {
    hash_double_float(h, (<span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span>)value);
    <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span>;
  }
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Hash as fixnum.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  {
    <span style="color: #00bfff;">const</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">long</span> <span style="color: #98f5ff;">double</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">limit</span> = ldexpl(1.0L, ECL_FIXNUM_BITS-ECL_TAG_BITS-1);
    <span style="color: #00bfff;">if</span> ((value==truncl(value)) &amp;&amp; (value &lt; limit) &amp;&amp; value &gt;= -limit) {
      hash_word(h, (<span style="color: #98f5ff;">cl_fixnum</span>)value);
      <span style="color: #00bfff;">return</span>;
    }
  }
  <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">/* </span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;">Hash as long double in dyadic form -- this is both for compatibility with
     bignums and to avoid hashing padding bits on x87.</span><span style="color: #7f7f7f;"> */</span>
  hash_lfloat(h, value);
}
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
And.. that's all. Complex numbers are hashed the same - when the complex float
imaginary part is zero then we hash only the real part, otherwise we hash both
parts. Summing things up, here are the dominant costs of the procedure:
</p>

