<div class="outline-2" id="outline-container-org8189bc0">
<h2 id="org8189bc0"><span class="section-number-2">4.</span> Benchmarks - knockout</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2" id="text-4">
<p>
I initially planned to run ad hoc benchmarks and get over with it, but I had too
much fun writing up tests and coming up with interesting benchmark profiles and
fell into another rabbit hole. In this section we'll compare ECL with itself (of
course), but we will also discuss other implementations.
</p>

<p>
First let's focus on tests. I've devised a few profiles for comparisons:
</p>
<dl class="org-dl">
<dt>:AVERAGE</dt><dd>we pick randomly rational and float numbers</dd>
<dt>:TYPICAL</dt><dd>fixnums, fixnum uuids, small floats and bignums in form 2<sup>n</sup></dd>
<dt>:DYADIC </dt><dd>rational and float numbers with overlapping values being =</dd>
<dt>:COLLIDE</dt><dd>white-box test that exploits casting LONG-FLOAT to DOUBLE-FLOAT</dd>
<dt>:BEYOND </dt><dd>white-box test that tests dyadic rationals beyond LONG-FLOAT range</dd>
<dt>:FIXNUM :BIGNUM :RATIO :DOUBLE</dt><dd>basic type-specific benchmark</dd>
</dl>

<p>
All benchmarks first generate 2<sup>16</sup> keys according to their profile and prefill
the hash table in the setup phase to avoid resizing. Only after that we run the
benchmark. Each profile has the same number of keys that are randomized during
setup. Each test is run 8 times to average results.
</p>

<p>
We measure both run time and consed bytes. The benchmark itself runs SETHASH and
GETHASH for each key in the collection, so the hash table implementation impacts
cross-implementation comparisons. On the bright side, it also reveals when there
are systematic collisions between hashes.
</p>

<p>
Here is an interesting story (relevant to these charts!). While analyzing data
I've noticed that "ECL-round2" unexpectedly conses for three profiles: :DYADIC,
:TYPICAL and :AVERAGE. I've felt very disappointed with these, because I went to
great lengths to <span class="underline">avoid</span> consing. The investigation revealed that if we don't
mix in these tests floats and rationals then consing is consistently zero. That
was the "aha!" moment - hash table allocates memory when it compares float and
rational keys! I'm keeping the figures as-is, but keep in mind that "ECL-round2"
hashing really doesn't cons; allocations come from the key comparison. I <i>could</i>
pull out my yak shaver and fix EQUALP too, but this descent stops being funny.
</p>

<p>
ECL variants are: "warmup", "round one" and "round two" that correspond to
approaches discussed earlier: cast everything to double, type-specific hashes,
and the final version where we exploit a dyadic canonical form. All tests are
performed using the new hash function (xxhash) and hash table (swiss).
</p>

<p>
Tested implementations are CCL, ECL, LispWorks and SBCL. There are caveats:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>I initially planned to include ABCL and CLASP too, but a smoke test revealed
<a href="https://github.com/armedbear/abcl/issues/763">ABCL#763</a> and <a href="https://github.com/clasp-developers/clasp/issues/1876">CLASP#1876</a>. Both share the same flaw as ECL before changes
described in this post, that is they don't hash numbers correctly under
EQUALP. Benchmarking them would be beside the point.</li>

<li>LispWorks performs badly on profiles any float numbers and other results are
dwarfed by it, so I've excluded LW from some plots (but accounted for it in
relevant analysis)</li>

<li>CCL is the mirror image of LispWorks, and performs badly on profiles with
rationals (and it is great with floats), so it is also skipped on some plots</li>

<li>The :COLLIDE test does not apply to other implementations because they don't
have LONG-FLOAT that is separate from DOUBLE-FLOAT - we use :TYPICAL instead</li>
</ul>

<p>
The smoke test:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-lisp"><code>(<span style="color: #00bfff;">defvar</span> <span style="color: #4eee94;">*ht*</span> (make-hash-table <span style="color: #f08080;">:test</span> #'equalp))
(setf (gethash 1/4 *ht*) 42)
(<span style="color: #00bfff;">unless</span> (eql (gethash 0.25 *ht*) 42) <span style="color: #7f7f7f;">; a proper test should cover more pairs
</span>  (format t <span style="color: #deb887;">"ERROR: 1/4 and 0.25 do not share the hash value under EQUALP.~%"</span>))
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
All charts report either elapsed time or allocated memory aggregated for the
whole execution. Smaller is <span class="underline">better</span>. Test results are normalized to [0, 1]
relative to the slowest / most consing variant in each panel. "0.00" means
"below 0.005" of the maximum, not zero. All measurements were performed on
<code>X86-64 Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-9900K CPU @ 3.60GHz</code>.
</p>

<p>
One of the hardest parts in analyzing data is to decide what we can remove
without simplifying the picture too much. Interpretations presented in this
section skip many benchmarks to present an understandable story.
</p>

<p>
If you are curious whether new ECL excels at complex floats (it does), or
whether it is true that small doubles and fixnums do not cons (they don't), then
you can dive straight into the <a href="https://turtleware.eu/sxhash-equalp-files/results.lisp">raw data</a> or, if you don't trust my numbers, then
you may inspect the of the <a href="https://turtleware.eu/sxhash-equalp-files/setup-number.lisp">number generator</a>.
</p>
</div>
<div class="outline-3" id="outline-container-org3eeb8ee">
<h3 id="org3eeb8ee"><span class="section-number-3">4.1.</span> ECL vs ECL</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-4-1">
<p>
First we will compare ECL variants developed in this post. The first chart shows
a benchmark for basic types:
</p>


<div class="figure" id="org8a7b49a">
<p><img alt="equalp-ecl-basic-types.png" src="https://turtleware.eu/sxhash-equalp-files/equalp-ecl-basic-types.png" />
</p>
<p><span class="figure-number">Figure 6: </span>(ECL-warmup ECL-round1 ECL-round2) x BASIC-TYPES x (TIME CONS).</p>
</div>

<p>
A few interesting observations emerge from this chart:
</p>

<ol class="org-ol">
<li>Hashing doubles is fastest on "ECL-warmup", because this implementation is
simplest for that particular profile. It also doesn't cons. "ECL-round1" is
dominated by memory allocations for large double values that are bignums.</li>

<li>Hashing ratios has progressively better results with each round, both the
time and consing.</li>

<li>Hashing bignums is a mirror image to hashing doubles, where "ECL-round1"
excels at bignums, "ECL-warmup" must coerce bignums to doubles and memory
allocation dominates its time. "ECL-round2" is as performant as "ECL-round1".</li>

<li>Fixnums on all variants are on a relatively fast path and don't need to cons
at all.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Notice that "ECL-round2" is slower only when we hash specifically doubles.
Otherwise it is always tied or it is the fastest. It also doesn't cons.
</p>

<p>
It already looks good, but charts start to look even more interesting when we
start exploiting mixed data patterns:
</p>


<div class="figure" id="org5ea71ac">
<p><img alt="equalp-ecl-mixed-data.png" src="https://turtleware.eu/sxhash-equalp-files/equalp-ecl-mixed-data.png" />
</p>
<p><span class="figure-number">Figure 7: </span>(ECL-warmup ECL-round1 ECL-round2) x MIXED-DATA x (TIME CONS).</p>
</div>

<ol class="org-ol">
<li><p>
The "beyond" profile is a benchmark where we generate bignums and ratios that
can't be represented as float, but have a shape that resembles floats - both
have a mantissa that can be stored in a double and an exponent that doesn't
fit in binary128 floats (the most precise float on ECL aarch64 and no tested
Lisp has a wider float).
</p>

<p>
And it shows. "ECL-warmup" takes the bait and collapses everything to double.
That spikes both allocations and collisions. "ECL-round1" avoids part of that
cost because bignums are hashed as is and they avoid collapsing to a single
hash (double positive infinity). "ECL-round2" avoids both allocation and the
collision traps and it is 400x faster (0.3s vs 125s) and doesn't cons while
"ECL-warmup" conses 2GB.
</p></li>

<li>The "collide" profile is similar, but numbers can be neatly represented as a
long float (binary80) and not as a double float (binary64). That means in
particular that both ratios and bignums must be hashed in a compatible manner
to floats. Once again "ECL-round2" is very fast (600x faster! 0.2s vs 118s)
and doesn't cons, while "ECL-warmup" conses 833MB. "ECL-round2" has much less
collisions than "ECL-warmup" thanks to exact bignum hashing.</li>

<li>The "dyadic" profile mixes fixnums, double floats, bignums in double float
range and dyadic ratios in double float range. This profile forces the need
to bring all numbers to the same canonical representation and does not
exhibit any systematic collisions.  Since all here boils down to doubles,
"ECL-warmup" conses less than "ECL-round1", yet "ECL-round2" is even better
thanks to our optimizations. Little consing that is exhibited comes from
comparing floats and bignums with EQUALP (not from hashing).</li>

<li>The "average" profile takes rational and float numbers at random. Here we can
again see that "ECL-round1" was not a regression over "ECL-warmup" - it was a
targeted optimization that tried to balance effort between different types of
numbers. Still, "ECL-round2" wins clearly against both variants.</li>
</ol>

<p>
My takeaways here are that:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>specialized implementations are better for their particular data profile,
notably "ECL-warmup" excels at doubles; "ECL-round1" ties "ECL-round2" at
bignums and balances other types well</li>

<li>"ECL-round1" approach optimizes for typical cases, but it is not very frugal
with memory (to the point where allocations dominate the benchmark in doubles)</li>

<li>"ECL-round2" also optimizes for typical cases but it is also austere with
memory allocations and avoids hash collisions - that is responsible for two
orders of magnitude faster and leaner implementation for adverse profiles</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div class="outline-3" id="outline-container-org0636259">
<h3 id="org0636259"><span class="section-number-3">4.2.</span> ECL vs the world</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3" id="text-4-2">
<p>
Comparing implementations between each other is inherently noisy. They don't
share the same hash table implementation and various unrelated operations on the
hashing and hash table path have different cost profiles. That said with a big
set of data we can clearly see some trends. Let's start with basic types:
</p>


<div class="figure" id="org82591e2">
<p><img alt="equalp-all-basic-types.png" src="https://turtleware.eu/sxhash-equalp-files/equalp-all-basic-types.png" />
</p>
<p><span class="figure-number">Figure 8: </span>(CCL ECL LispWorks SBCL) x BASIC-TYPES x  (TIME CONS).</p>
</div>

<p>
LispWorks is excluded from the test involving floats because it is around 2500x
slower than CCL (96s vs 0.04s) and conses 66MB while other implementations don't
cons at all. In benchmarks involving rational numbers it is competitive. My
guess is that LispWorks calls RATIONALIZE on floats and that get costly fast.
</p>

<p>
CCL is another side of the same coin. It performs well for floats (around 1.7x
slower than ECL and SBCL), but it is much worse for rationals. The difference is
not that drastic but still much slower for bignums compared to LispWorks (0.18s
vs 0.03s). It is surprising but it allocates data even for FIXNUMs. It probably
calls FLOAT on all numbers before hashing.
</p>

<p>
SBCL heavily optimizes fixnums, bignums, and doubles - it is a leader on these
basic types and it doesn't cons. It drops a ball with ratios where it is slower
and conses 155MB (while ECL nor LispWorks don't allocate memory for ratios).
</p>

<p>
ECL never conses and wins for ratios, while for other basic types it is always
second. For BIGNUM SBCL pulls ahead, while for FIXNUM and DOUBLE it follows
closely.
</p>


<div class="figure" id="orgae09c88">
<p><img alt="equalp-all-mixed-data.png" src="https://turtleware.eu/sxhash-equalp-files/equalp-all-mixed-data.png" />
</p>
<p><span class="figure-number">Figure 9: </span>(CCL ECL LispWorks SBCL) x MIXED-DATA x  (TIME CONS).</p>
</div>

<p>
Results in these benchmarks are implicit from tests on basic types:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>BEYOND, DYADIC and AVERAGE involve floats, so LispWorks is too slow to
include. SBCL is also underperforming on these tests, because these profiles
contain ratios - the cost seems to be dominated by memory allocations.</li>

<li>TYPICAL profile is manageable for all implementations, but both CCL and
LispWorks underperform because both (small) floats and rationals are in the
test set. CCL allocates much more memory than others. SBCL performs well, but
is slower than ECL.</li>

<li>In all these tests with mixed numbers ECL is the leader and allocates least
(as noted earlier, hashing itself doesn't cons at all).</li>
</ul>

<p>
My takeaway is that each implementation could close the gap to the others if:
</p>
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>LispWorks adds hashing specialization for floats</li>
<li>CCL adds hashing specialization for rationals</li>
<li>SBCL adds hashing specialization for ratios</li>
</ul>

<p>
This new approach of ECL is competitive with all implementations on each metric
and it is either the first or a close second with the biggest (around 1.5x) gap
to SBCL hashing bignums.
</p>
</div>
</div>
